{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"tail -50 agent/report.md; sed -n '1,100p' agent/verify_chronos_prefix.py; git diff --stat; du -sh agent/results/chronos_forecast_cache\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"tail -50 agent/report.md; sed -n '1,100p' agent/verify_chronos_prefix.py; git diff --stat; du -sh agent/results/chronos_forecast_cache\"","aggregated_output":"The seeded starter is a constant-volatility lognormal random walk. Shortening volatility memory alone yields small, inconsistent improvements for 24-hour paths. The seasonal model improves all 36 asset/format/window cells in two initial validation windows: two scored days ending September 16 and two ending October 10.\n\nFiltered empirical innovations retain substantial tails after volatility normalization, but are weaker than the selected mixture for one-hour CRPS. Large residual kurtosis alone does not establish a useful forecast change.\n\nA global 15% width reduction failed, as did a finer seven-day seasonal profile and a fitted range/volume residual correction. Shortening the daily fast-memory half-life to three hours also failed (only 6/26 cells improved), although a 15-minute fast half-life improved all ten one-hour cells on the full windows. The residual model improved a future-volatility proxy but reversed sign between September and October on actual CRPS. This distinction matters: predicting volatility more accurately under a surrogate loss does not necessarily improve the submitted distribution.\n\nLognormal path-level volatility uncertainty (log standard deviation 0.35, unit second moment) improves one-hour crypto and commodity/equity forecasts. It worsens crypto 24-hour forecasts, where we retain Gaussian increments. The selected mixture improves all 39 affected stress-test cells. On a BTC/SOL component audit, both official price and volatility CRPS improve, but 76–83% of the absolute gain comes from price CRPS. The much larger percentage improvement in the smaller volatility term should not be mistaken for its contribution to the total. Minute-of-hour profiles add a smaller consistent crypto gain: 19/20 initial cells improve. Sobol sampling has small, seed-dependent effects and is not selected universally.\n\nCalendar kernels use June–August events as independent observations, pooling assets within each release and shrinking estimates. Simultaneous releases combine by maximum rather than multiplication. Prospective tests cover later CPI/PPI, FOMC and payroll windows: 49/54 cells improve, with all nine configuration/window averages favorable. Holiday suppression is not selected. A July–September profile used only after its training cutoff improves all eight commodity/equity assets on the full October window (mean per-asset gain 0.629%). Reducing seasonal shrinkage from 10% to 2% improves 9/10 full-window crypto 24-hour cells; it is retained only for that configuration. Stronger event kernels improve 34/36 full-window cells and all six configuration/window means. The combined model passes canonical float32 validation. Commodity/equity forecasts additionally use ten-minute seasonal bins, shrunk halfway to their half-hour parent, and causal profile refreshes. Fine resolution plus refresh improves all16 cells over each individual change. COM-only Sobol sampling improves15/16 further cells; three-seed testing also supports its modest benefit. Adding latent-volatility reversion on top of Sobol worsens both windows and is discarded.\n\nA small public pretrained model, Chronos-T5-tiny, forecasts hourly deseasonalized variance from context alone. A quarter-blend improves 9/10 initial one-hour cells but slightly worsens crypto 24-hour results. It also improves 9/10 cells beyond the stronger fast-memory baseline (mean per-asset gains 0.153% and 0.144%). A matched hourly-volatility persistence control worsens both windows. Tiny improves all10 full-window cells beyond v3, but a144-hour median control beats it in9/10 initial-window cells. Full-window median validation reverses that short-window result: median loses9/10 cells against tiny, with mean per-asset regressions0.057%/0.326%. Tiny therefore beats this robust control on the broader dates, although performance is not uniform across subperiods. A matched GARCH blend loses9/10 cells against tiny. Chronos-small fails the predeclared both-window mean criterion against tiny despite8/10 favorable cells. Offline revisions, latency and reproducibility checks are recorded in the journal. Fair fresh-process1000-path benchmarks measure tiny/small cold responses8.30/8.38s and warm responses0.232–0.241/0.306–0.309s. The original small-model5s cold gate failed; the journal records an explicit pre-scoring amendment separating the constitution’s60s hard response bound from its5s practical throughput target.\n\nHistorical validation uses the official backtester scoring worker on cached validator prompt times. It exactly reproduces ordinary backtest CRPS on three representative parity checks. With profiles fitted only through July1, seasonality improves 33/36 July/August asset-window cells and all six configuration means. These runs make no field-rank claim.\n\nThe refined COM model benefits from reducing the seasonal variance floor from10% to2%:24/24 asset/window cells improve across the two full windows and an additional eight-day September period. A weekend-only reduction loses to the global reduction. Adding5% uniform narrowing then improves13/16 full-window cells and7/8 added-period cells. The resulting COM combination is retained. Crypto24h also retains5% narrowing after all five assets improve in the added period; one-hour narrowing remains under interaction testing.\n\nA variance-clock Brownian bridge improves one-hour Sobol integration while preserving the target increment covariance exactly. It improves19/20 initial cells across two seeds, all10 default-seed full-window cells, all five added-period cells, and all10 causal historical cells. It is selected only for one-hour crypto; direct Sobol remains the COM sampler and crypto24h retains ordinary random sampling.\n\nAn initially promising state-dependent volatility mixture improves every development full-window cell and beats a training-matched constant mixture. It nevertheless fails the historical August mean because of XRP losses in hot volatility states. The failure persists with bridge sampling, so the symmetric adaptation was withdrawn from serving. Its narrower-center control is slightly better on development dates, and a calm-only alternative also fails the strict historical mean criterion. These outcomes caution against interpreting consistent recent gains as universal evidence.\n\nSmall fitted return correlations improve recent crypto CRPS slightly but fail causally refitted historical tests, so they are discarded. Correcting Gaussian paths to have flat arithmetic-price expectation also fails the two-window criteria; zero log-return centering remains selected.\n\nThe crypto24h5% width reduction is not universal: it improves the older July window but worsens August by0.3170% in mean per-asset relative CRPS. Across all five disjoint periods, its pooled official CRPS is4219.2935 versus4231.2708 at scale1 (3840 prompts,0.2831% lower). It also improves all20 full-window cells under two additional seeds. We retain this large, simple aggregate correction with its counterexample, rather than claiming uniform historical dominance. New adaptive/covariance additions faced stricter historical gates; this difference in selection standards is explicit in the journal.\n\n## Results\n\n![Candidate v9 CRPS reductions at 1000 paths](figures/candidate_v9.png)\n\nThese are official CRPS means, averaged equally across assets within each configuration, at 1000 paths. The current provisional candidate v9 combines seasonal EWMA, crypto within-hour profiles, stronger calendar kernels, group-specific volatility mixtures, faster one-hour memory, lower crypto 24-hour seasonal shrinkage, and refreshed robust commodity/equity variance forecasts. It improves all 36 asset/format/window cells in the two ten-day comparisons. The composed results use scale95_v5 for crypto24h, floor02_scale95_v7 for commodities/equities, and bridge_v8_full for crypto1h, all at float32 precision. Exact serving/reference parity passed36 asset/date/format calls at17 paths with research environment overrides deliberately ignored.\n\n| Scored days | Configuration | Starter | Provisional v9 |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_24H | 4296.6726 | 4201.6155 |\n| Sep09–18 | COM_EQU_24H | 2316.0261 | 2080.2779 |\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_1H | 960.4266 | 923.5471 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_24H | 3531.2042 | 3406.1522 |\n| Oct01–10 | COM_EQU_24H | 2065.4112 | 1783.6086 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_1H | 741.2317 | 690.6868 |\n\nFor the earlier v1 checkpoint, paired circular two-day block resampling gives descriptive intervals below zero in all six configuration/window combinations; these intervals do not correct for repeated model selection. That checkpoint improves all 20 daily configuration means, although individual asset-days can regress (HYPE one-hour on Sep17 is one example). Current mean per-asset percentage reductions are 2.39%, 9.57%, 4.12% in September and 3.65%, 13.51%, 7.06% in October, in table configuration order. Ratios of the aggregate means differ because those give greater influence to assets with larger raw CRPS. These are development-inclusive validation windows, not untouched holdouts or the unseen campaign score.\n\nOn the available CPU, v9 generates1000 paths in0.0179 seconds for BTC one-hour,0.0565 seconds for AAPLX24h and0.0607 seconds for XAU24h, excluding context loading. The latter contexts contain3715/898 missing closes and produce valid paths. Exact36-call reference parity,17- and1000-path contracts, repeatability and seed sensitivity passed.\n\n## Limitations\n\nValidation includes two ten-day windows, release stress windows, and earlier causal-profile checks, but substantial repeated model selection remains. Adjacent 24-hour prompts overlap substantially; prompt count is not independent sample size. Seasonal profiles may drift as these young perpetual markets mature. Cached validator paths and minute candles differ in missingness and stale prices. Event types have few independent dates, and some release effects cannot be separated. Float32 versus float64 baseline output changes full-window cell means by at most 0.000012%; selected model gains are much larger. Two additional seeds improve all 72 asset/window cells for the earlier v3 checkpoint. No rank improvement is used as evidence of model improvement.\n\n## What more budget is for\n\nFinish learned-model interactions against the bridge-only model, their causal historical validation, and measured return-autocorrelation experiments. The floor and24h width choices already passed added-period checks; one-hour adaptive width was rejected after an added-period reversal. Both baseline and earlier v1 already passed36-cell float32 comparisons; maximum cell-mean precision effects are about0.000012%. The final choice will follow official paired CRPS.\n\"\"\"Verify short decoding preserves the seeded forecast prefix exactly.\"\"\"\nimport os,random,time,json\nimport numpy as np,pandas as pd,torch\nfrom synth_lib.preparation.config import default_store_root\nfrom predict import load_minute_prices,context_end\nfrom research_model import shape_at\nfrom chronos_vol import pipeline\nseed=int(os.environ.get('SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED','20261011'))\nmodel=pipeline();rows=[]\nfor asset,date in [('BTC','2026-09-16T00:02:00Z'),('HYPE','2026-10-08T13:17:00Z')]:\n t=pd.Timestamp(date);frame=load_minute_prices(default_store_root(asset).parents[2],asset,t-pd.Timedelta(days=8),t)\n end=context_end(t,3600);frame=frame.loc[(frame.index>end-pd.Timedelta(days=7))&(frame.index<=end)]\n ret=np.log(frame.close.astype(float)).diff();rv=ret.pow(2)/shape_at(frame.index,asset,True)\n hourly=rv.resample('h').mean();count=frame.close.resample('h').size()\n hourly=hourly[(count==60)&(hourly.index+pd.Timedelta(hours=1)<=frame.index[-1]+pd.Timedelta(minutes=1))].iloc[-144:]\n values=hourly.to_numpy();center=float(np.nanmedian(values));series=np.log(np.maximum(values,center*.001)/center)\n forecasts={};elapsed={}\n for length in [25,3]:\n  random.seed(seed);torch.manual_seed(seed)\n  if torch.cuda.is_available():torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(seed)\n  started=time.perf_counter()\n  with torch.inference_mode():pred=model.predict(torch.tensor(series,dtype=torch.float32),prediction_length=length,num_samples=32)\n  forecasts[length]=pred.detach().cpu().numpy()[0];elapsed[length]=time.perf_counter()-started\n mean25=np.mean(np.exp(np.clip(forecasts[25],-3,3)),axis=0)*center\n mean3=np.mean(np.exp(np.clip(forecasts[3],-3,3)),axis=0)*center\n origin=hourly.index[-1]+pd.Timedelta(hours=1)\n max_index=int((t+pd.Timedelta(hours=1)-origin).total_seconds()//3600)\n row={'asset':asset,'start_time':str(t),'sample_prefix_equal':bool(np.array_equal(forecasts[25][:,:3],forecasts[3])),'mean_prefix_equal':bool(np.array_equal(mean25[:3],mean3)),'max_mean_difference':float(np.max(np.abs(mean25[:3]-mean3))),'required_forecast_values':max_index+1,'seconds':elapsed}\n rows.append(row);print(json.dumps(row),flush=True)\nassert all(r['sample_prefix_equal'] and r['mean_prefix_equal'] and r['required_forecast_values']<=3 for r in rows)\n .gitignore                                         |  2 ++\n agent/journal.md                                   | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++\n agent/research_model.py                            |  8 ++++++++\n .../chronos_adaptive_v8_full/XRP_1h_2026-09-19.log |  3 +++\n agent/results/chronos_adaptive_v8_full_suite.log   |  3 ---\n agent/results/forecast_cache_parity.log            |  2 +-\n .../historical_heat_scale/HYPE_24h_2026-08-22.log  |  3 +++\n .../historical_heat_scale/XRP_24h_2026-08-22.log   |  3 +++\n agent/results/historical_heat_scale_suite.log      |  2 ++\n agent/results/queue_heat_scale.log                 |  3 +++\n 10 files changed, 47 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)\n9.3M\tagent/results/chronos_forecast_cache\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
