,asset,time_increment,c.get('_calendar_path',''),c.get('holidays',False),c.get('event_fine',False),c.get('event_power',1.),c.get('event_exclude',()),c.get('event_table'))\nagent/research_model.py:238:        z=correlated_normals(z,float(table(c.get('return_ar1_table','autocorrelation.json'))[asset][str(lag)]))\nagent/research_model.py:241:        quantiles=np.asarray(table('innovations.json')[asset][str(lag)])\n# DATA.md — the shape of your market data (generated from THIS snapshot at setup)\n\nEach day is one parquet file, `date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, a full 1440-row minute grid\nwith columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close` is the scored series.\n`close` is the venue's 1-minute candle close (the last trade of that minute); the other\ncolumns describe the same minute. For the Hyperliquid-routed assets open/high/low are\nderived from 1-second closes, so a minute's range is sampled rather than continuous.\n`source`/`ingested_at`/`is_final` are ingestion provenance — ignore them. Semantics:\n\n- **NaN close** = no trade that minute (thin market or feed gap). NOT market hours:\n  these venues trade 24/7. Real density varies by asset and era — see the table.\n- **Missing file** = before that market's first trade. Nothing existed; nothing is hidden.\n- Scoring drops NaN per prompt. Never interpolate: inventing prices in the gaps is how\n  you fool your own validation.\n\nPer-asset coverage, measured from this snapshot (nan% = share of NaN minutes among the\nasset's real days; first/last 30d columns show density drift — early months of newer\nlistings are genuinely thin, low realized volatility there is real):\n\n| asset | first day | last day | real days | nan% total | nan% first 30d | nan% last 30d |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AAPLX | 2025-11-21 | 2026-10-11 | 325 | 41.1% | 60.1% | 27.0% |\n| BTC | 2025-08-01 | 2026-10-11 | 437 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n| ETH | 2025-08-01 | 2026-10-11 | 437 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n| GOOGLX | 2025-11-18 | 2026-10-11 | 328 | 23.2% | 23.9% | 2.1% |\n| HYPE | 2025-08-01 | 2026-10-11 | 437 | 0.1% | 0.1% | 0.0% |\n| NVDAX | 2025-11-12 | 2026-10-11 | 334 | 10.3% | 14.9% | 6.3% |\n| SOL | 2025-08-01 | 2026-10-11 | 437 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n| SP500 | 2026-03-18 | 2026-10-11 | 208 | 1.0% | 2.8% | 0.9% |\n| SPCX | 2026-05-17 | 2026-10-11 | 148 | 2.9% | 4.6% | 6.3% |\n| TSLAX | 2025-11-13 | 2026-10-11 | 333 | 14.8% | 26.2% | 16.5% |\n| WTIOIL | 2026-01-06 | 2026-10-11 | 279 | 11.3% | 58.8% | 0.6% |\n| XAU | 2025-12-22 | 2026-10-11 | 294 | 10.5% | 39.2% | 7.1% |\n| XRP | 2025-08-01 | 2026-10-11 | 437 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n\n`timestamp` loads as `datetime64[us, UTC]`. An integer view of it (`.view(\"int64\")`) counts in that unit:\nderive minute indices from the dtype, never by assuming nanoseconds.\n\n**Cached realized paths** (13 assets): **2026-06-20 → 2026-10-11**. Where a\nprompt's slice of the price store contains NaN, scoring substitutes the validator's own\narray for that prompt. Outside this range it cannot, and the prompt is scored on the\npoints that remain — which is a smaller CRPS from a thinner reference, not a better model.\n\nSteps with no price change in those paths, the arrays CRPS is computed against: XAU (5 min) 11.3%, SOL (1 min) 9.9%, XRP (1 min) 8.0%, SPCX (5 min) 6.8%, BTC (1 min) 5.8%, AAPLX (5 min) 5.4%, NVDAX (5 min) 5.4%, SP500 (5 min) 5.2%, SOL (5 min) 4.1%, GOOGLX (5 min) 4.0%, TSLAX (5 min) 3.3%, XRP (5 min) 3.0%, ETH (1 min) 2.9%, HYPE (1 min) 1.3%, WTIOIL (5 min) 0.9%, BTC (5 min) 0.7%, ETH (5 min) 0.4%, HYPE (5 min) 0.3%.\n\n**Scheduled events** (`calendar/events.parquet`; your champion reads it at `$SYNTH_BENCHMARK_CALENDAR`):\n200 events, 2025-08-01 → 2027-01-18: every date\npublished by the cutoff, upcoming ones included. Columns: `time_utc`, `event`, `scope` (`all`, or the\nassets it concerns, comma-separated), `detail`, `source`. Times are scheduled New York times in UTC,\nso they move an hour with daylight saving: US macro data at 08:30, FOMC statements at 14:00, the EIA\npetroleum report Wednesdays at 10:30 (holiday weeks differ). Earnings are anchored at the session,\n08:00 before the open and 16:00 after the close; an NYSE closure at the 09:30 open that does not\nhappen, an early close at 13:00. A date published or moved after the cutoff is not here.\n\nEvents of each type before and after the snapshot's last day, and the other types released at the\nsame minute, whose effects cannot be told apart from it there:\n\n| event | before cutoff | after cutoff | at the same time as |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CPI | 13 | 1 | — |\n| EARNINGS | 18 | 0 | — |\n| EIA_PETROLEUM | 62 | 3 | — |\n| FOMC | 9 | 2 | — |\n| GDP | 13 | 1 | PCE 12/14 |\n| NFP | 14 | 0 | RETAIL_SALES 2/14 |\n| NYSE_CLOSED | 11 | 4 | — |\n| NYSE_EARLY_CLOSE | 2 | 2 | — |\n| PCE | 14 | 1 | GDP 12/15 |\n| PPI | 13 | 1 | RETAIL_SALES 3/14 |\n| RETAIL_SALES | 15 | 1 | NFP 2/16, PPI 3/16 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'leverage|asym|drift|antithetic|jump|Student' agent/journal.md; sed -n '110,275p' agent/research_model.py; tail -3 agent/results/timesfm_v9_full_suite.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'leverage|asym|drift|antithetic|jump|Student' agent/journal.md; sed -n '110,275p' agent/research_model.py; tail -3 agent/results/timesfm_v9_full_suite.log\"","aggregated_output":"31:Hypothesis: a Gaussian with a point volatility forecast understates both return tails and uncertainty in realized volatility. Compare Student-t innovations (df=5, unit variance) and lognormal path-level volatility uncertainty (log std=0.35, unit second moment), each added separately to seasonal EWMA.\n73:Iteration 2 result: path volatility mixture improves all 10 crypto1h asset-window cells; equal-asset relative gains are 0.293% in September and 0.535% in October versus seasonal Gaussian. All four daily group means improve, although individual asset-days can regress. Student-t is weaker (0.168% and 0.332%). For crypto24h both tails models slightly worsen the equal-asset means (mixture +0.098%/+0.019%, Student +0.121%/+0.012%). Verdict: KEEP mixture as a candidate for crypto1h, DISCARD blanket heavy tails for crypto24h. Com/equ mixture and Student both look favorable but need broader validation. Full numbers in results/tails_comparison.txt and raw paired artifacts.\n97:Hypothesis: range, volume/trade count, leverage, or faster realized-volatility ratios may predict residual scale errors after the seasonal fast EWMA. Before spending official backtests, fit an interpretable ridge correction to future mean absolute returns, using January–July for training and August as a diagnostic holdout. All price labels end before September 1. Collapse the target into a one-hour scale; use six features derived only from past data. This proxy is NOT CRPS and cannot justify promotion.\n113:Iteration 7 preliminary diagnostic: range/volume/all features reduce August proxy MSE, with rough log-width-based potential around 0.17%/0.26%/0.44% CRPS; leverage alone worsens the proxy. Memory ratios alone explain approximately 0.26%, so the incremental all-feature opportunity is smaller, approximately 0.18%. Before official testing, discovered training EWMA used unlimited history while serving only sees seven days. Fix the diagnostic training to exact finite-seven-day weighted moments and shift future targets to reflect the one-hour prompt's issuance lead. The first diagnostic is provisional and superseded on rerun. Serving correction will omit the fitted intercept because a global 15% narrowing already failed; test only conditional residual variation.\n119:Iteration 7 corrected diagnostic: exact seven-day EWMA plus issuance-aligned target gives August proxy MSE 0.110870 for memory ratios and 0.104188 for all features, against 0.119771 constant residual. Range 0.114133, volume 0.110360, leverage 0.120198. Training/serving feature checks pass for all five crypto assets, max absolute discrepancy <4.7e-13. Verdict: official tests warranted for all-features and memory-only corrections; discard leverage-alone study. Neither proxy number is a CRPS result.\n191:Kill criterion: skip simulation work if correlations and aggregation ratios are negligible; otherwise require official CRPS improvement across multiple windows. Do not infer predictive drift from a variance-ratio diagnostic.\n220:Hypothesis: standardized observed returns retain tails and central concentration beyond Gaussian increments, potentially improving the one-hour realized-volatility distribution. Before implementing bootstrap, measure June–August returns divided by a strictly lagged seasonal EWMA scale: kurtosis, tail probabilities, and central absolute quantiles. Compare minute and five-minute samples. These diagnostics are not CRPS and cannot promote an innovation model. If excess tails survive filtering, test symmetric empirical resampling with unit variance; preserve zero drift, same volatility forecast and official 1000-path scoring. Reject if gains fail either window; do not assume a large kurtosis implies useful CRPS improvement.\n238:## Iteration 20 — paired antithetic sampling with volatility mixtures\n240:Hypothesis: pairing each complete innovation vector with its negative reduces finite-ensemble location error. For a volatility mixture, each pair must share the same volatility draw; otherwise the intended symmetry is lost. Implement this pairing only behind a research option, preserving Gaussian/mixture distributions and environment-derived seeds. Compare candidate v1 against paired antithetic on BTC/SOL/AAPLX/SP500, both formats where applicable, two initial windows, three seeds (20261011,11,37). This is a sampling change, not extra market information. Require favorable pooled mean in each configuration and consistency across seeds before promotion; report any one-hour volatility-score tradeoff. Do not select based on one seed.\n278:Hypothesis: the selected mixture gives each path one constant latent volatility for its whole horizon. A stationary mean-reverting log-volatility process can preserve every base-step mixture marginal while allowing uncertainty to change over time. Replace the constant latent normal with an AR(1) Gaussian state, half-life one quarter of the forecast horizon (15min for1h,6h for24h), same stationary std0.35 and unit expected variance multiplier. Preserve the original initial latent draw and innovation draws for a closer comparison. No return drift or cross-asset inputs are introduced.\n298:Iteration20 three-seed result: paired antithetic pooled mean changes are COM-0.0384%, crypto24h+0.0202%, crypto1h+0.0582%. Crypto24h flips from a default-seed gain to losses under11/37; one-hour worsens on every seed's pooled mean. DISCARD universal antithetic sampling. COM-only effect is small and cross-asset inconsistent (AAPLX/SP500); retain random sampling pending broader evidence rather than selecting a tiny pooled improvement from two assets.\n580:## Iteration59 — asymmetric calm-state correction\n586:Iteration58 confirms the counterexample under bridge sampling: July improves0.0723%, August regresses0.0871%, with XRP+0.73% and only3/5 August cells improved. DISCARD symmetric adaptive mixture from serving. Preserve it as a research candidate and keep its completed favorable and unfavorable results. V9 is revised to v8+one-hour bridge ONLY before nomination; the earlier adaptive freezing step is explicitly withdrawn. The asymmetric calm-state hypothesis is a separate registered experiment and earns no promotion from this failure.\n600:Use the prevalidation shrunken correlations in a stationary Gaussian AR(1) innovation law, preserving unit variance at every individual step and zero forecast mean. Keep the observed-price start, volatility forecasts, mixtures and sampling unchanged. No conditional-return drift is added. Apply only when the requested step matches the fitted1-/5-minute lag; other step sizes retain independent innovations.\n616:Iteration59 new-date result also fails:July mean+0.0059%(0/5 wins),August-0.1580%(3/5). DISCARD calm-only adaptation; do not rescue it using the favorable August average. This completes the planned asymmetric follow-up without a new serving change.\n636:Hypothesis: zero-mean Gaussian log returns induce an upward arithmetic-price expectation, particularly for volatile24h paths. Test subtracting one half of each path's cumulative conditional log-return variance, so the underlying Gaussian-mixture process has flat conditional arithmetic-price expectation. Preserve every volatility forecast, random draw, mixture and sampler. This is one centering convention, not a fitted directional signal or drift grid.\n638:Use v9 without return correlation, Student innovations or empirical innovations; those require different conditional-moment arguments and are explicitly incompatible with this experiment. Compare all three configurations on both full windows,1000 paths,float32. Require both means and most cells before retaining a group; any retained group needs added-period validation. Four workers are released by completed covariance studies.\n        'return_signed':np.clip(np.nansum(ret[-60:])/np.sqrt(60*v60*season[-1]+eps),-4,4),\n    }\n    return vals\n\ndef simulate(asset,start_time,time_increment=300,time_length=86400,num_simulations=1000,context_prices=None):\n    seed=int(os.environ.get('SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED','20261011'))\n    rng=np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    config=json.loads(os.environ.get('MODEL_CONFIG','{}'))\n    config['_calendar_path']=os.environ.get('SYNTH_BENCHMARK_CALENDAR','')\n    config['_seed']=seed\n    return simulate_config(asset,start_time,time_increment,time_length,num_simulations,context_prices,rng,config)\n\ndef simulate_config(asset,start_time,time_increment,time_length,num_simulations,context_prices,rng,c):\n    steps=time_length//time_increment\n    short=time_length<=3600\n    seasonal=c.get('seasonal',False)\n    shape_name=c.get('table','seasonality.json')\n    if c.get('refresh',False):\n        available=context_prices.index[-1]+pd.Timedelta(minutes=1)\n        if available>=pd.Timestamp('2026-10-01',tz='UTC'):\n            shape_name=c.get('refresh_table','seasonality_oct01.json')\n    if c.get('refresh_schedule'):\n        available=context_prices.index[-1]+pd.Timedelta(minutes=1)\n        selected_end=table_end(shape_name)\n        for name in c['refresh_schedule']:\n            trained=table_end(name)\n            if selected_end<trained<=available:\n                shape_name=name;selected_end=trained\n    close=context_prices.close.astype(float)\n    valid=close.dropna();last=float(valid.iloc[-1])\n    # A lag of 1 minute for 1h and 5 minutes for 24h matches the base scored increments.\n    lag=c.get('lag',1 if short else 5)\n    r=np.log(close).diff(lag)\n    good=np.isfinite(r.to_numpy())\n    rv=r.to_numpy()[good]**2/lag\n    idx=close.index[good]\n    weights_shape=shape_at(idx,asset,seasonal,shape_name,c.get('seasonal_shrink',.1))\n    if c.get('clock',False):weights_shape*=clock_at(idx,asset,lag,c.get('clock_table','clock.json'))\n    rv=rv/weights_shape\n    age=np.asarray((pd.Timestamp(start_time)-idx).total_seconds())/60\n    if len(rv)<10:\n        rv=np.array([1e-8]);age=np.array([1.0])\n    def estimate(half):\n        w=np.exp2(-age/half)\n        raw=np.sum(w*rv)/max(np.sum(w),1e-12)\n        blend=c.get('robust',0.)\n        if blend:\n            correction=float(table('robust_factors.json')[asset][str(lag)])\n            absolute=(np.sum(w*np.sqrt(rv))/max(np.sum(w),1e-12))**2*correction\n            return (1-blend)*raw+blend*absolute\n        return raw\n    if c.get('estimator','ewma')=='constant':\n        initial=long=float(np.mean(rv))\n    else:\n        long=estimate(c.get('long_half',4320))\n        if short:\n            initial=.6*estimate(c.get('fast_half',60))+.25*estimate(360)+.15*long\n        else:\n            initial=.5*estimate(c.get('fast_half',720))+.5*long\n    if c.get('_variance_diagnostics') is not None:\n        c['_variance_diagnostics'].update(initial=float(initial),long=float(long))\n    future=pd.date_range(pd.Timestamp(start_time)+pd.Timedelta(seconds=time_increment),periods=steps,freq=pd.Timedelta(seconds=time_increment))\n    sf=shape_at(future,asset,seasonal,shape_name,c.get('seasonal_shrink',.1))\n    if c.get('clock',False):sf*=clock_at(future,asset,max(1,time_increment//60),c.get('clock_table','clock.json'))\n    if c.get('events',False):\n        event_index=future+pd.Timedelta(seconds=60 if c.get('event_bar_alignment',False) else 0)\n        sf*=event_factor(event_index,asset,time_increment,c.get('_calendar_path',''),c.get('holidays',False),c.get('event_fine',False),c.get('event_power',1.),c.get('event_exclude',()),c.get('event_table'))\n    decay=np.exp2(-np.arange(1,steps+1)*time_increment/60/c.get('revert_half',720 if not short else 240))\n    variance=(long+(initial-long)*decay)*sf*time_increment/60\n    if c.get('garch',False):\n        if not short or not seasonal:raise ValueError('GARCH experiment requires seasonal one-hour mode')\n        from garch_vol import forecast\n        if c.get('chronos',False):raise ValueError('GARCH/Chronos combination requires an explicit ensemble design')\n        learned_variance=forecast(context_prices,asset,future,shape_name,c.get('clock',False))*sf*time_increment/60\n        fraction=c.get('garch_blend',1.)\n        if fraction==1.:variance=learned_variance\n        else:variance*=1-fraction+fraction*np.clip(learned_variance/np.maximum(variance,1e-14),.25,4.)\n    if c.get('chronos',False) or c.get('hourly_persistence',False):\n        from chronos_vol import forecast_variance\n        learned=forecast_variance(context_prices,asset,future,c['_seed'],c.get('hourly_persistence',False),c.get('chronos_model','chronos-t5-tiny'),shape_name=shape_name)\n        base=np.maximum(long+(initial-long)*decay,1e-14)\n        if c.get('_forecast_diagnostics') is not None:\n            c['_forecast_diagnostics'].update(base=base.copy(),learned=learned.copy())\n        blend=c.get('chronos_blend',.25)\n        variance*=1-blend+blend*np.clip(learned/base,.25,4.)\n    if c.get('timesfm',False):\n        if not short or c.get('chronos',False) or c.get('garch',False) or c.get('hourly_persistence',False):\n            raise ValueError('TimesFM study is an independent one-hour model')\n        from timesfm_vol import forecast_variance\n        learned=forecast_variance(context_prices,asset,future,c['_seed'],shape_name)\n        if learned is not None:\n            base=np.maximum(long+(initial-long)*decay,1e-14)\n            variance*=.75+.25*np.clip(learned/base,.25,4.)\n    scale=c.get('scale',1.0)\n    if isinstance(scale,dict):scale=scale.get(asset,1.0)\n    if c.get('heat_scale',False) and not short and asset in ['BTC','ETH','SOL','XRP','HYPE']:\n        heat=np.clip(np.log(max(initial,1e-14)/max(long,1e-14))/np.log(2.),0.,1.)\n        scale=scale+(1-scale)*heat*decay\n    sig=np.sqrt(np.maximum(variance,1e-14))*scale\n    if short and c.get('residual'):\n        params=residual_parameters()[c['residual']]\n        features=residual_features(context_prices,asset)\n        x=np.array([features[k] for k in params['features']])\n        standardized=np.nan_to_num((x-params['mean'])/np.asarray(params['std']))\n        correction=float(standardized@np.asarray(params['coef'][1:]))\n        sig*=np.exp(c.get('residual_strength',.5)*np.clip(correction,-.25,.25))\n    n=num_simulations\n    mixture_normal=None\n    if c.get('sampler') in ['sobol','sobol_bridge']:\n        from scipy.stats import qmc\n        from scipy.special import ndtri\n        engine=qmc.Sobol(d=steps+1,scramble=True,seed=int(rng.integers(0,2**32-1)))\n        u=engine.random_base2(int(np.ceil(np.log2(max(n,1)))))[:n]\n        normal=ndtri(np.clip(u,1e-12,1-1e-12))\n        if c.get('sampler')=='sobol_bridge':\n            from qmc_bridge import bridge_normals\n            mixture_normal=normal[:,:1]\n            z=bridge_normals(normal[:,1:],sig)\n        else:z=normal[:,:steps];mixture_normal=normal[:,-1:]\n    elif c.get('sampler')=='stratified':\n        from scipy.special import ndtri\n        ranks=rng.uniform(size=(n,steps)).argsort(axis=0)\n        z=ndtri((ranks+.5)/n)\n    elif c.get('antithetic',False) or c.get('paired',False):\n        z=rng.normal(size=((n+1)//2,steps));z=np.concatenate([z,-z])[:n]\n    else:z=rng.normal(size=(n,steps))\n    if c.get('return_ar1',False) and time_increment==lag*60:\n        from path_dependence import correlated_normals\n        z=correlated_normals(z,float(table(c.get('return_ar1_table','autocorrelation.json'))[asset][str(lag)]))\n    if c.get('empirical',False):\n        from scipy.special import ndtr\n        quantiles=np.asarray(table('innovations.json')[asset][str(lag)])\n        u=2*np.abs(ndtr(z)-.5)\n        z=np.sign(z)*np.interp(u,(np.arange(len(quantiles))+.5)/len(quantiles),quantiles)\n    df=c.get('df',0)\n    if df:z=z/np.sqrt(rng.chisquare(df,size=z.shape)/df)*np.sqrt((df-2)/df)\n    mix=c.get('mix',0)\n    if mix and (c.get('adaptive_mix',False) or c.get('adaptive_center_only',False)):\n        if c.get('adaptive_mix',False) and c.get('adaptive_center_only',False):\n            raise ValueError('Adaptive mixture and center-only are separate experiments')\n        disagreement=np.log(max(initial,1e-14)/max(long,1e-14))\n        if c.get('adaptive_mix')=='calm':disagreement=min(disagreement,0.)\n        adaptive_width=min(.55,float(np.sqrt(mix*mix+.10*disagreement*disagreement)))\n        if c.get('adaptive_center_only',False):sig*=np.exp(mix*mix-adaptive_width*adaptive_width)\n        else:mix=adaptive_width\n    if c.get('mix_scope')=='non_crypto24h' and not short and asset in ['BTC','ETH','SOL','XRP','HYPE']:\n        mix=0\n    path_volatility=1.\n    if mix:\n        # Multiplicative path-level volatility uncertainty, E[scale^2]=1.\n        if mixture_normal is None:\n            if c.get('paired',False):\n                half=rng.normal(size=((n+1)//2,1));mixture_normal=np.concatenate([half,half])[:n]\n            else:mixture_normal=rng.normal(size=(n,1))\n        if c.get('mix_revert',False):\n            from scipy.signal import lfilter\n            half_seconds=time_length*c.get('mix_half_fraction',.25)\n            rho=2**(-time_increment/half_seconds)\n            shocks=rng.normal(size=(n,steps))*np.sqrt(1-rho*rho)\n            shocks[:,0]=mixture_normal[:,0]\n            mixture_normal=lfilter([1.],[1.,-rho],shocks,axis=1)\n        path_volatility=np.exp(mix*mixture_normal-mix*mix)\n        z*=path_volatility\n    shocks=z*sig\n    logs=np.column_stack([np.zeros(n),np.cumsum(shocks,axis=1)])\n    if c.get('price_martingale',False):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
