{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_262","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md agent/BUDGET.md && tail -100 agent/journal.md'","aggregated_output":"# CAMPAIGN campaign-6 — constitution (gpt-6-astra)\n\nYou are a research engineer working this problem on your own. No one is available to answer\nquestions while the run is in progress, so make the call yourself and record it in your journal.\nKeep working until the landing order arrives. What you are working on:\n\n> A calibration problem: build the price-simulation model with the lowest CRPS you can reach. CRPS measures how well your predicted distribution matches what actually happened, and lower is better. Your model must handle all three scoring configurations (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H) and is measured over the 10 days following your data cutoff. Judge every experiment on CRPS; the constitution explains why the field-relative position is a poor target. Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n\n## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n\nSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\nasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\ncompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\nminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\nnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n\n## The three scoring configurations\n\nThe subnet scores three configurations across two horizons (its code calls them *competitions*;\n`synth.validator.competition_config` is the source of truth). Your model has to handle all three —\na `simulate()` that ignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon breaks on\ncrypto-1h.\n\n| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 (+ SPYX, retired — see the breaks table) | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n\nBTC, ETH, SOL, XRP and HYPE are in two competitions: look one up by `(asset, time_length)`\n(`synth_lib.backtester.config.competition_for`), never by asset. A 24h prompt scored with the\ncrypto-1h intervals is wrong by a large, silent margin.\n\n**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\nwhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\nargument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\nyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n\nScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\nThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n\n## What you are optimising\n\n**CRPS is the objective. Lower is better.** It is the only quantity that measures your model\ndirectly: it compares your predicted distribution against what actually happened, prompt by\nprompt, and it does not depend on what anyone else submitted.\n\nA field-relative position is also reported, and it is tempting to optimise because it looks like a\nresult. **Do not.** The submissions are tightly packed, and the per-prompt score the aggregation\nconsumes is your CRPS *minus the lowest CRPS on that prompt* — which re-bases a small difference\nonto a much smaller number. A tenth of a percent of CRPS can therefore move a position by a\nhundred places in either direction. That makes position a loud, unstable readout of a tiny change:\nuseful for checking you are in the right regime, useless as a target. Two runs of the same model\ncan land far apart on it without differing in any way that matters.\n\nSo: **judge every experiment on CRPS**, per configuration and per asset. Report position if you\nlike, but never trade a CRPS regression for a better one.\n\nHow the reported position is formed, for completeness: the aggregation does not average across\nassets. It weights each asset by `ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` (`synth.validator.moving_average`),\nnormalizes per miner, then applies a softmax, so a high-coefficient asset carries much more\nweight. Your champion is measured over the **10 days following your data cutoff**, with each of\nthe three configurations contributing one third regardless of asset count. The moving average\nbackfills a model that appears late with the worst value in the set, so generation starts one\nhorizon before the window and yours is never treated as late.\n\n## Your resources\n\n- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n  `agent/predict.py --days N --eval-end D` answers the prompts the validator scored in the N days\n  before D, at the start times it actually used, read from the offline field bundle: the same\n  kind of prompts your champion is scored on. `--eval-end` may be any day up to your cutoff, and\n  each run scores only its own predictions, so runs can go in parallel. It is also used for the 1h\n  format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment 60`). `--cadence-minutes N` answers an even\n  grid instead; a cadence that divides an hour puts every prompt on the round hour, where\n  volatility spikes, so do not read an absolute calibration level off such a grid.\n- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n  trade_count`, resolve it with\n  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n  **2025-08-01 → 2026-10-11**, mounted\n  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n\n  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n\n  | From | Assets |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n\n  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, NaN semantics (NaN close = no\n  trade that minute, NOT market hours; missing file = before the market's first trade), and a\n  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index).\n\n  **The venue never closes; the underlying does.** Minutes exist around the clock for every asset,\n  so there is no session gap in the DATA — but the equity and commodity perps track markets that\n  shut, and their VOLATILITY follows those hours closely. Measured on the snapshot: realized\n  volatility per minute swings about 4-5x across the UTC day for the tokenised equities and gold,\n  peaking at the US cash open and bottoming overnight, and weekdays run 2-4x hotter than weekends.\n  The crypto assets, genuinely 24/7, are far flatter on both. A constant per-asset volatility is\n  therefore too wide overnight and too narrow at the open, and CRPS charges for both. Measure the\n  seasonality yourself before you model it, and check whether it holds for each asset rather than\n  assuming one shape fits the group.\n\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs comparing**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n  2026-06-23 configuration split (breaks table below) constrains only FIELD-RELATIVE numbers —\n  never compute one on a window that starts before it. CRPS itself is unaffected.\n\n  **Four structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | 2026-09-22 | crypto-1h scoring added a volatility term. A crypto-1h CRPS from before it is not comparable with one after; `agent/predict.py --latest-formula` scores earlier prompts with it too, which keeps CRPS comparable (field ranks before the date then are not). |\n  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **`market_data/calendar/events.parquet`** (its path is in `$SYNTH_BENCHMARK_CALENDAR`): scheduled\n  events — US macro releases (CPI, NFP, PPI, PCE, retail sales, GDP), FOMC statements, NYSE closures\n  and early closes, the EIA weekly petroleum report, and the earnings of the companies behind the\n  tokenised stocks — with every date published before your cutoff, upcoming ones included. DATA.md\n  gives its columns, its span, the count of each type and which share a release minute. Your miner\n  gets the same file in production, refreshed daily: read it inside `simulate()` through the env\n  var, never from a copy or a hardcoded path, and expect an event type or a date to be missing.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact CRPS function, and can also place you among the recorded field. Use it\n  via `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement the metric. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE here:\n  `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle of\n  historical scores inside your snapshot, so no API call is needed. DATA.md gives the measured\n  span of scored prompts it holds: a field-relative number outside that span has nothing to\n  compare against, though CRPS is still computable. DATA.md also gives the span of the cached\n  realized paths, which are a different thing — they stand in for minutes your price store is\n  missing.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  `to` names a whole UTC day, inclusive: a query to 08-16 also returns 08-16's rows.\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; copying the paths served by an individual miner is\n  FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, copying an\n  individual miner's served paths is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP n1-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 60GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA T4 (16GB VRAM; Turing, no bf16). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **Parallel work shares this envelope.** Every agent or job you start — subagents, workflows,\n  background runs — spends from the same credit budget and runs on the same machine. The budget in\n  `BUDGET.md` and the hardware above are totals, whatever a tool's own guidance says about token\n  cost.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n  **A champion is MANDATORY.** What is scored is how strong your champion is, not whether you dared\n  to nominate one: ending with no `agent/CHAMPION` is a Score of 0 and a wasted leg — strictly worse\n  than nominating something imperfect.\n\n## Constraints (they are what make the result valid)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-10-11** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-10-11 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   invalidates the result. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n   Event dates come only from that calendar: a table typed from memory or fetched from the web goes\n   stale and wrong where your miner serves.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). One implementation of the metric is what makes results comparable.\n4. Not allowed: falsifying the journal or results; reproducing another participant's served\n   output as your own.\n5. **Keep binary and encoded data out of your terminal output.** Read parquet with pandas and\n   `.npy` with numpy — never `cat` a parquet, a git bundle, or model weights, and never pipe\n   base64 into your context. A screenful of encoded bytes buys you nothing, costs context, and\n   is a known trigger for content classifiers that will end your turn.\n6. **Start from public sources only**: synth-lib, synth-subnet, the Synth API, and your own\n   ideas. The operator's existing miner models are not part of your materials and must not\n   inform your work. Nothing of that kind belongs in your environment; if any of it turns up\n   there, leave it unread and note it in `suggestions.md`.\n7. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n8. **If your model uses randomness at all, seed it from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED`.** Nothing\n   here asks you to introduce randomness — a deterministic champion ignores this rule. If you\n   do use it, read the variable once at the top of `simulate()` and seed every generator your\n   model touches from it: Python's `random`, NumPy, torch, and anything carrying its own. It\n   is absent during your own runs and set only at evaluation, so fall back to a seed of your\n   choosing when it is missing. What you may never do is hardcode a seed that ignores the\n   variable, or leave a generator unseeded so the clock or OS entropy supplies it.\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested **CRPS per configuration**,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths, alongside the CRPS of\n  whatever you started from — a nomination without measured numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot. Lessons earlier agents paid for:\n\n- **Price a residual before fitting it.** For a Gaussian fan, expected CRPS at scale σ against\n  the right σ* is about 1 + ¼·(ln σ/σ*)² times the optimum: a ±20 % global scale error costs about\n  1 %, and a width correction across buckets can gain at most about ¼ of the variance of the\n  log width ratios. A minute of arithmetic tells you whether a day of sweeping can pay.\n- **Test a feature by what it adds to a fast EWMA of realized volatility**, not by its own\n  predictive power. Range estimators, volume, trade counts and the leverage effect act on future\n  volatility through realized volatility, which the EWMA has already priced. What it cannot hold\n  is information outside the context: the calendar, and anything older than the 7 days\n  `simulate()` receives.\n- **Validate an event effect leave-one-event-out.** A type occurs 8 to 12 times a year, so a\n  multiplier fitted and tested on the same releases is in-sample.\n- **Check what `simulate()` can see before building on it**: one asset's 7-day context per\n  call, nothing else. A feature that needs another asset's current prices cannot be served.\n- **Count the asset-window cells where a change helps**, across disjoint windows, not only its\n  average: a small gain signed the same way in most cells survives; a larger one that flips sign\n  between windows is noise.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 87.42 / 100.00 USD (87%)\n- Time   : 4.8 h elapsed, 13.2 h remaining (26%)\n- Phase  : landing\n\n## LANDING ORDER — NON-NEGOTIABLE\n\nYour envelope is nearly exhausted. STOP any new experiment NOW.\n1. Finalize `agent/journal.md` (including the last iteration).\n2. Write the complete `agent/report.md` (honest results, failures included).\n3. Nominate your champion: write the `agent/CHAMPION` file (sha, agent_dir, profiles, notes).\n   The path matters: the harness looks ONLY at `agent/CHAMPION`.\n4. Verify that the weights/artifacts required by `simulate()` are in the workspace.\n\n\n## Harness notes\n\n- GPU: 78 min busy so far, peak memory 0.5 GiB\n\nBudget72.55/100USD,14.0h remaining,normal. Hypothesis: conditional variance reversion should account for elapsed time since the latest usable return,including issuance lead and missing trades. Official context_end is2minutes before start for1h and3minutes for24h. EWMA normalization cancels a common age offset,while the current forward decay starts at forecast time. Test adding max(age_of_last_valid_return,0) to every forward reversion elapsed time. This is a forecast-timing hypothesis,not a claim that an empirically calibrated EWMA is a literal latent-state filter.\n\nPreserve all observations,estimator weights,seasonality,calendar,path starting prices,mixture and sampler. Do not insert extra price shocks or interpolate missing prices. Compare v9 alone with this single timing policy across all36 full-window cells,1000paths,float32,four workers. Require both means and most cells per configuration;retained groups need added-period/causal historical checks. No combined nonlinear model before the independent effect is established. Residual validation queues reserve8workers,so maximum12.\n\n## Iteration82 — directly measure the unselected learned-mixture interaction\n\nThe completed cached Chronos+bridge+symmetric-mixture combination improves10/10full cells(mean September920.9922,October688.7061;mean per-asset-0.2508%/-0.3069%)and4/5addedSeptember cells(mean922.0894,-0.0660%). The components each fail historicalAugust,but that does not mathematically establish the combination's historical result. Resolve this uncertainty directly rather than implying an unmeasured combined failure. This does not reverse either component's rejection or permit recent scores to override their gates.\n\nRun the unchanged combination with pre-July tables on the sameJuly/August historicalwindows,using existing exact-input cached forecasts and cache-only mode. TwoCPU workers,zeroGPU inference,total maximum14with the residual and timing studies. Require BOTH historicalmeans andmost cells to improve against bridge v9;any retained combination additionally needs the newJuly/August date pair before selection. No coefficient,timing,blend or threshold changes. This is a distinct interaction test,not tuning the failed components against the historical losses.\n\nIteration80 completed:tree addedSeptember improves5/5cells(mean922.0836,-0.0653%),historicalJuly improves5/5(mean766.5914,-0.0579%)butAugust regresses(+0.0142%,1681.8958,2/5wins). Ridge addedSeptember improves3/5(mean922.4911,-0.0166%),July5/5(mean766.5141,-0.0652%)butAugust regresses(+0.0958%,1682.3433,2/5wins). DISCARD both under the unchanged historical criterion. The tree's stronger proxy and development results do not establish regime transfer.\n\nIteration82 direct combined test also FAILS:July improves5/5(mean766.1906,-0.1117%),August worsens(mean1686.2416,+0.1706%,2/5wins),includingXRP+0.98%. DISCARD the combined Chronos/adaptive bridge candidate on its own measured evidence. Its failure is now observed,not inferred from component failures.\n\n## Iteration83 — supervise conditional correction with official loss slopes\n\nBudget74.14/100USD,13.9h remaining,normal. Hypothesis: the mean-absolute-return surrogate omits features of the official price/volatility CRPS objective. Reuse the same six features,fixed depth2/80tree learner and matched ridge,but supervise with finite-difference official CRPS slopes around the unchanged baseline. This is a new target,not retuning the failed proxy correction against historical outcomes.\n\nFirst collect paired1000-path,float32 official CRPS at log-width shifts-0.1,0,+0.1 on the existing April–August rotating-origin diagnostic dataset,using pre-July seasonal/clock/event tables. Each origin's three fans use the same benchmark seed. Scoring calls agent.predict.generate and agent.predict.cached_backtest(the official synth-lib worker);no metric is reimplemented. Use only complete snapshot reference paths at these exact origins,record excluded missing-reference counts,and never fill prices. No field rank is computed. Four CPU workers,one asset per worker;each asset processes its three widths sequentially.\n\nFit onApril–July only. Divide each official finite-difference slope by that asset's training mean baseline CRPS;target=-2*slope,clipped[-3,3]. The fixed factor2 is a gradient-step convention,not a fitted score optimum. Center both predictions over training data to isolate conditional correction,then retain the same half-strength±0.25log-scale serving clip. Compare both models by ACTUAL official CRPS onAugust origins,not by squared gradient prediction error. Require lower aggregate mean and most assets;tree additionally must beat ridge. Only a passing model earns a September-profile refit and full/recent/causal historical/independent-seed tests. All fits,means and normalizers for this first gate excludeAugust labels. Repeated use ofAugust as a development gate will be disclosed;it is not an untouched test.\n\nIteration83 causality amendment BEFORE any label scoring:pre-July artifacts are unavailable atApril–June origins. Even training-loss predictions must obey the campaign's prediction-time rule. Restrict the first official-label dataset toJuly–August,train onJuly,and evaluate onAugust. Keep the same rotating origins and all preregistered learner/target settings. A passing model will refit onJuly–August labels generated by this CAUSAL pre-July forecasting policy,then be tested against v9 with its current profiles;no retrospective scoring with September-fitted profiles onJuly inputs. A causal historical fitting-policy test,if reached,will first need independently fitted pre-April artifacts andApril–June official labels. No results have been observed under the abandoned April-label plan.\n\nIteration81 full result FAILS allgroups:September mean per-asset changes crypto24h-0.0005%,crypto1h-0.0018%,COM approximately-0.0000%;October reverses to+0.0004%,+0.0052%,+0.0004%. DISCARD observation-age adjustment without changing the reversion rate or adding price shocks. The effects are small and inconsistent;an appealing timing interpretation alone does not improve the empirical model.\n\nIteration83 label collection completed:2,476exact origins×3widths=7,428official1000-path scores. July training has1,236origins;August holdout has1,240. Bothnormalizers and learner fitting useJuly only. JSON traversal parity maxerror2.78e-16. ActualAugust officialCRPS evaluation oftree/ridge is running with fourCPU workers and exactpaired baseline subsets. No decision is based on gradient-prediction MSE.\n\nIteration83 ACTUAL official August holdout gate PASSED for both models: baseline787.3886,gradient-tree786.8555(mean per-asset-0.0876%,4/5wins),gradient-ridge787.2394(-0.0402%,4/5wins). Tree beats ridge in4/5cells(-0.0473%). XRP worsens for both; no per-asset exception is allowed. These are exact-origin complete-reference development holdout scores, not field ranks or the unseen evaluation.\n\n## Iteration84 — refit loss-supervised correction before recent validation\n\nRefit the identical slope target, normalization rule, tree/ridge settings and centering on all2,476July–August official labels. Every label was generated with artifacts available byJuly1; no retrospective use ofSeptember-fitted forecast parameters. Serving residual features retain the pre-July table recorded by their artifact, while the underlying forecast uses the current v9 profiles. This profile-update transfer is part of the policy being tested, not assumed equivalent to the training baseline.\n\nVerify exported inference and1000-path serving, then score both full windows against v9, fourCPU workers per model. Require both means/most cells for each;tree must additionally beat ridge before preferring it. Any retained model needs addedSeptember, independent seeds11/37, and a truly causal historical fitting-policy audit using pre-April artifacts andApril–June official loss labels. No hyperparameter changes after seeing these outcomes.\n\nIteration84 refit completed on2,476official-label origins; exported inference error4.44e-16. Current-base parity and all six real-context tree/ridge1000-path checks pass, including exact repeats and positive/finite paths. Warm generation0.0216–0.0247s. Both full-window studies now run with four workers each; serving remains v9 pending their results.\n\n## Iteration85 — another causal three-month COM refresh\n\nBudget78.19/100USD,13.7h remaining,normal. Hypothesis: the already-selected rolling seasonal estimator benefits from a further update three days after its latest tested refresh as these markets mature. Fit the identical30-minute parent and50%-shrunk10-minute child onJune17–September16 andJuly8–October7. Add these September17/October8 cutoffs to the COM-only refresh schedule; all other v9 settings remain fixed.\n\nScore both variant and matched v9 control on two fully affected scored-day windows:September18–19 andOctober9–10(two days endingSep20/Oct11),1000paths,float32. Require both means and most asset cells. If retained, test a separately preregisteredSeptember25 refresh(June25–September24) on the addedSeptember period. Do not select one favorable refresh date or tune lookback/binning. This tests the existing fitting policy at newer cutoffs, not a new seasonal estimator. Only after temporal confirmation would a final cutoff refit be considered. Sequential fitting/control/candidate queue uses at most4workers; the two gradient studies use8,maximum12.\n\nIteration84 full result: gradient-tree PASSES both means,923.5471→922.4810 and690.6868→690.1906(mean per-asset-0.1015%/-0.0819%,8/10wins),and beats ridge10/10. Gradient-ridge FAILS October(+0.0802%,0/5wins)despiteSeptember-0.0324%;DISCARD ridge. Tree remains provisional.\n\n## Iteration86 — seed and causal-history audit of loss supervision\n\nFreeze the retained gradient-tree. Test addedSeptember against sep_late_bridge,and independent seeds11/37 against newly scored frozen-v9 controls on both full windows. Require added mean plus all four independent-seed means and most cells favorable.\n\nFor the historical fitting-policy audit,fit fresh crypto seasonal/clock artifacts onFebruary–March endingApril1. Fit event multipliers fromOctober2025–March2026 using only the supplied calendar and snapshot,endingApril1;this supplies enough prior releases without borrowing future event fits. Collect official±0.1log-width labels onApril–June origins using those pre-April artifacts and1000paths. Recompute all six features with the pre-April table;do NOT reuse the pre-July-normalized feature columns from the origin schedule. Fit the same gradient-tree onthese labels with training-only normalizers,then evaluate July/August using the current causal pre-July baseline and the artifact's pre-April feature transform. This mirrors the recent training-policy/profile-update transfer and avoids retrospective label lookahead.\n\nRequire both historical means and most cells to improve against historical_bridge_1h. No target,tree,shrinkage or clipping changes. Run one four-worker seed/late queue and one sequential historical-fitting/label/scoring queue capped at four,alongside the COM refresh queue capped at four;maximum12. No GPU is needed.\n\nIteration85 refresh result FAILS:September1772.4210→1771.0679(mean per-asset-0.0880%,6/8wins),October1757.6093→1757.9295(+0.0067%,4/8wins). DISCARD the extra refresh policy;do not keep justSeptember17 or perform the conditionalSeptember25 test. The selected COM schedule remains unchanged,and this result cautions against an unmeasured final-cutoff refresh.\n\n## Iteration87 — separate ordinary seasonality from scheduled-event windows\n\nHypothesis: ordinary seasonal estimates can absorb scheduled-event variance that is then added again by the selected calendar forecast. Refit only the COM seasonal profiles after excluding five-minute returns whose real-time intervals overlap an already-selected non-unit event kernel. Event types,scopes,dates and kernel windows come from the supplied calendar and the committed event_multipliers.json; do not invent dates or tune exclusions. Keep event multipliers themselves fixed to isolate this single fitting-policy change.\n\nApply the same exclusion to parent30-minute and child10-minute fits at all existing selected cutoffs(Sep1,Sep14,Oct1,Oct5),with unchanged three-month windows,parent shrinkage,estimator and final floor. Record excluded fractions before scoring. Params must be available by each fit cutoff. This is a hypothesis about double-counting,not an assertion that the current calibrated model is wrong.\n\nCompare all16 full-window COM cells against v9,1000paths,float32. Require both means and most cells; any retained policy must pass the addedSeptember period before serving. Do not combine with the rejected extra-refresh schedule or refit event multipliers in this experiment. Sequential fitting/scoring queue uses at most4workers;gradient recent/history queues reserve4each,total12.\n\nIteration86 added-period result FAILS the raw-CRPS mean criterion:gradient-tree922.5773→922.6097,despite a favorable mean per-asset percentage change(-0.0055%)and3/5wins. These aggregations answer different questions. The declared added configuration mean is raw official CRPS; do not substitute the favorable percentage average after seeing the result. DISCARD promotion. Finish the already-registered independent-seed and causal historical audits for the record. No claim that loss supervision robustly improves v9 is warranted from the two development windows and August gate alone.\n\nIteration86 causal-history result also FAILS:gradient-treeJuly767.0085→766.6364(mean per-asset-0.0518%,5/5wins),August1681.5828→1684.2893(+0.0745%,3/5wins;XRP+0.68%). Pre-April fits andApril–June official-label provenance are retained. Seed11 recent means improve0.1020%/0.0822%(8/10wins),so the observed temporal failure is not explained by that alternative fan seed. Seed37 completes as registered.\n\nIteration87 event-window exclusion FAILS both COM means:2080.2779→2081.6011(+0.0817%,0/8wins),1783.6086→1784.0238(+0.0242%,2/8wins). Excluded return fractions span0.545%–0.792% across32asset/profile fits; overlap boundary gate passed. DISCARD this seasonal-only change with event multipliers fixed. This does not establish the result of a jointly refitted seasonal/event model.\n\n## Iteration88 — match the supervision comparison\n\nThe proxy-versus-gradient studies differ in training dates and profile transforms as well as target. Do not attribute their performance difference solely to loss supervision. Fit the original log-mean-absolute-return proxy on the exact1,236July/1,240August complete-reference origin set used for the gradient study,with the same pre-July features,tree80/depth2/min_leaf100 settings,centering and half-strength clipping. Refit onthe same2,476July–August origins for full-window comparison. Only target construction changes within this matched comparison.\n\nScore theJuly-trained proxy tree onAugust exact origins,and its refit onboth full current-v9 windows,1000paths,float32. Compare to the corresponding gradient-tree and v9 controls. This is primarily a controlled attribution experiment. Any standalone retained candidate still requires favorable holdout/full means and most cells,then added-period/causal-history/seed validation;do not select it solely because it beats a rejected gradient model. TwoCPU scoring workers; no GPU.\n\nIteration88 August matched-target result:proxy mean786.8545 versusgradient786.8555,essentially tied in raw CRPS;proxy improves all5assets versusbaseline787.3886. Againstgradient it wins3/5assets,but its mean per-asset percentage difference is+0.0074%. These differing aggregates and tiny raw difference do not support attributing the earlier August gain specifically to official-loss supervision. Full matched comparison continues.\n\n## Iteration89 — re-estimate calendar kernels with matched seasonal backgrounds\n\nHypothesis: the failed event-window exclusion may require calendar multipliers estimated against its changed background. Fit two fresh COM event parameter sets using the same pre-September data,calendar,event-date pooling,shrinkage and leave-one-event-out diagnostics: one against the unmasked fine seasonal profile at its serving floor0.02,and one against the masked fine profile atfloor0.02. Keep event power1.5 fixed. The unmasked kernel refit is a necessary control because the original kernels used a different seasonal normalization.\n\nScore both models against v9 on all16full-window COM cells,1000paths,float32. Also compare masked+refit versusunmasked+refit to isolate the exclusion policy. Require favorable raw means and most cells in BOTH windows;retained candidates need addedSeptember. Do not credit exclusion for a gain shared by the unmasked kernel refit. No iterative remasking,new event types,window tuning or fitted power change. Sequential diagnostic/fitting/scoring queue uses at most4CPUworkers;other active queues remain below14total.\n\n## Landing — finalize v9; no new experiments\n\nThe harness issued its non-negotiable LANDING ORDER at$85.00 and4.6h elapsed. Stop new experiments. Select the previously frozen v9 policy because it completed its registered validation. Do not promote late candidates that have only development/holdout evidence, and do not perform an unmeasured cutoff refit.\n\nIteration86 seed37 completed: recent mean per-asset improvements0.1019%/0.0819%,8/10wins. Both additional seeds support the recent gradient-tree effect, but its added-period raw-mean and causal historical failures remain decisive.\n\nIteration88 completed its already-running matched comparison during finalization:matched-proxy full means922.1880/689.5235,10/10wins versusv9,8/10wins versusgradient-tree. August raw CRPS essentially ties the gradient target. Verdict:RESEARCH CANDIDATE ONLY,not nominated;additional-period,historical and seed checks were not conducted before landing. Do not label it a failed temporal model or a fully validated improvement.\n\nIteration89 completed:unmasked fine-floor kernel refit worsens both COM means(+0.0491%/+0.0177%,1/16wins). Masked-profile plus kernel refit also worsens both(+0.1040%/+0.0328%,2/16wins;means2081.9863/1784.1507). DISCARD both. No new jobs were launched after the landing order;previously running jobs finished while deliverables were finalized.\n\nFinal selected serving behavior remains v9:crypto24h seasonal/calendar EWMA with0.95width and ordinary Gaussian sampling;COM robust fine-seasonal refreshed EWMA,0.02floor,0.95width,0.35mixture andSobol;crypto1h15-minute fast memory,240-minute variance reversion,0.35mixture andSobol Brownian bridge. No adaptive mixture,pretrained forecast,residual learner,PCA sampler,extra refresh or event-exclusion policy is active.\n\nFinal verification uses the exact official context slice. Exact36-call reference parity passed,as did17-/1000-path contracts,positive finite paths,correct starts,exact repeats andseed sensitivity. Warm1000-path times:BTC1h0.0163s,AAPLX24h0.0560s,XAU24h0.0578s. Sparse contexts contained3713/898NaN closes. All10required serving files are tracked/present,total485085bytes;no pretrained weights are required. Default dependency graph confirmsSciPy via properscoring/synth-subnet/synth-lib.\n\nFinal canonical aggregation checks confirm1000paths,float32,matching prompt counts,and36/36improved cells. September starter→champion:crypto24h4296.6726→4201.6155,COM2316.0261→2080.2779,crypto1h960.4266→923.5471. October:3531.2042→3406.1522,2065.4112→1783.6086,741.2317→690.6868. Ratios of raw configuration means improve2.2123%,10.1790%,3.8399% and3.5413%,13.6439%,6.8190%,respectively. Final report distinguishes these from averages of per-asset percentage changes and documents the retained width's historical exception.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
